WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:07.550
this is Jocko podcast number 78 with
echo Charles and me Jocko willing

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:21.330
good evening echo good evening nightmare
now that's a word that I use often times

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:26.330
to describe the most horrible things or
the most wretched things and in my

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:39.200
hierarchy of adjectives nightmare is
right alongside of Hell I'm not sure

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.800
which one is worse in my mind that's why
I say they're right alongside each other

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:50.270
because hell is beyond the worst thing
your mind can imagine and that's

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:57.739
basically what a nightmare is when
you're asleep and your mind delves into

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:04.920
the most horrific things beyond what you
can consciously and wilfully imagine in

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:16.670
your own head that's what a nightmare is
now what if there was a place that was

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:36.119
worse than a nightmare and it was a real
place who could create such a place and

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:46.409
the answer to that is we can
I shall never forget how I was roused

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:51.390
one night by the groans of a fellow
prisoner who threw himself about in his

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:58.649
sleep obviously having a horrible
nightmare since I had always been

00:01:58.649 --> 00:02:03.509
especially sorry for people who suffer
from fearful dreams or deliria I wanted

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:08.399
to wake the poor man suddenly I drew
back the hand which was ready to shake

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:15.569
him frightened at the thing I was about
to do at that moment I became intensest

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:22.200
intensely conscious of the fact that no
dream no matter how horrible could be as

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:28.920
bad as the reality of the camp which
surrounded us and to which I was about

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:39.900
to recall him and that's a quote from
the book man's search for meaning by

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:46.950
Viktor Frankl which will be appearing in
two today and it's a book about the Nazi

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:57.389
concentration camps which are really as
he pointed out they're worse than any

00:02:57.389 --> 00:03:04.889
nightmare we can imagine here's a
holocaust survivor

00:03:04.889 --> 00:03:15.480
eddie weinstein on the death and the
atrocities in Treblinka by now the

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:20.819
transports were coming every day one was
much like the next the same incidence

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:27.239
recurred again and again once as I swept
out the interior of a car an SS man

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:30.329
suddenly struck me with his whip and
ordered me to help a woman who was

00:03:30.329 --> 00:03:36.690
unable to stand and climb out of the car
the idea was to show his victims how

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:42.150
well the ill were being treated then he
ordered one of the workers to take the

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:48.989
women take the woman through the field
the field hospital for care the

00:03:48.989 --> 00:03:52.760
newcomers really believed that the alien
woman would

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:58.220
be taken to an infirmary none of them
realized that the field hospital was in

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:06.019
fact just a giant pit about 30 yards in
diameter which was always ablaze they

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:11.269
would place those who were sick or
disabled on the ground at the edge of

00:04:11.269 --> 00:04:17.239
the pit facing in the SS man and the
in-charge circulated among them and shot

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:23.060
them in the back of the neck then the
workers cast them into the pit some of

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:26.120
the victims were still breathing when
they were tossed in with the other

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:32.930
bodies the pit was separated from the
spacious field by a barrier of fresh

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:37.669
pine branches which will replace from
time to time to conceal the infirmary

00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:43.820
from the newcomers until the last moment
20 minutes after I helped the woman

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:48.800
climb out of the railroad car I was told
that some infants were sitting by the

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.840
pit and no one else was there because
the SS soldier had taken his lunch break

00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.169
I gathered some trash and went over
there I saw the woman who I had removed

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:04.010
from the car still breathing sitting at
the edge of the pit and staring and

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:10.160
fright into the burning Inferno and its
contents the half cremated bodies of old

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:17.270
people and children mixed with
smoldering trash she tried to stand up

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:23.449
but her legs would not hold her she
looked at the workers who stirred the

00:05:23.449 --> 00:05:32.690
embers remnants of human beings so they
would burn better nearby were about a

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:38.659
dozen infants too young to have learned
how to walk they were not crying they

00:05:38.659 --> 00:05:43.400
certainly did not understand what was
happening they looked about almost

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:49.880
certainly in hopes of finding their
mother or father later I heard that

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:53.599
right after he returned from his lunch
break the SS man shot them all and

00:05:53.599 --> 00:05:59.570
ordered the workers to throw the tiny
bodies into the flames of all my

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:05.450
memories of that accursed place the
vision of these babies is undoubtedly

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:10.020
the worst
I see their faces whenever I remember

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:18.270
Treblinka as I write these lines more
than 50 years after that day I still

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:32.390
cannot overcome the horror in another
report here Martin's pet of tar now

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:39.740
Poland describes the massacre of torn
out Jews in an interview

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:44.970
we heard the columns of Jews under
German escort at night it was going

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:50.160
constantly they were passing our house
because this was already on the

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.030
outskirts of the city the cemetery and
they were marching them to the woods

00:06:54.030 --> 00:07:01.500
behind the city as we found out later
they were all shot over there during the

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:06.330
day I looked out through the shingles my
father said I shouldn't look but anyway

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:12.480
I was a kid I was curious and the roof
was overlooking a cemetery and wagons

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:18.300
with bodies dead bodies were coming in
groups they were bringing in groups of

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:25.110
Jewish people that had to dig trenches
they had to dig ditches and the bodies

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:31.670
dumped in and after those Jews that dug
the ditches they were shot also and

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:37.170
pushed in by another group that came in
after them into those ditches and lime

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:42.300
was poured over the bodies and the next
group covered up those ditches and dug

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:45.050
other ditches

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:58.450
they brought in pregnant women and they
didn't use any bullets

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:11.540
they used bayonets the screams of the
mothers that their children they tore

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:20.830
their children out of their arms and the
screams of the children I still hear

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:35.900
and Solomon radowski who is the only
survivor from the 78 people in his

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:45.560
family described the concentration camp
they throw the people in the

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:55.070
crematoriums the children I will never
forget alive they throw them in the

00:08:55.070 --> 00:09:01.910
crematoriums they grabbed by an arm by a
leg by the head and throw them into the

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:12.050
ovens there it was so tragic the cries
and people when crying there you know it

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:21.709
was so terrible I can feel it now I can
even see the people the other people

00:09:21.709 --> 00:09:24.410
were crying the children were hollering
mama

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:38.320
daddy help me mama daddy help me it was
terrible

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:54.640
and one more report on Treblinka from
Henry gold and the worst thing that

00:09:54.640 --> 00:10:00.880
happens to a person in that instance he
loses self-respect and that's exactly

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:05.140
what happened to the majority of the
people some of them even lost their

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:09.730
minds they couldn't take it they
couldn't take the hunger they couldn't

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:14.610
take the beatings they couldn't take the
cold no clothing and the uncertainty and

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:19.210
the uncertainty I think that was the
worst thing that can happen to a person

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:24.700
you didn't know from one moment to the
next what was going to happen life was

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:29.440
very cheap a person was alive one minute
the next minute he was shot killed

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:37.720
beaten up beaten to death torn apart
apart by dogs and so on life was very

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:42.240
very cheap and that's the way the
Germans liked it

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:48.340
that particular camp was governed by the
Jewish Jewish police and so on the

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:55.480
Germans did not stay in the camp they
were on the outside but the life inside

00:10:55.480 --> 00:11:00.310
of the camp was just as bad without the
Germans being right there than if they

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:09.220
would be the Jewish police was I don't
know you take a person in that situation

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:13.960
and you give them a little power and he
becomes the same as the oppressor

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:20.860
himself there were some policemen that
were not bad but there were a lot of

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:27.130
them that were ruthless there were
others that would beat people and do all

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:29.640
kinds of things that would degrade them
and so on

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:35.530
especially women they cut their hair off
and they look like men they look like

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:40.480
scarecrows and they would get beaten up
by the Jewish police just as bad as the

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:48.970
man for no reason at all if they wanted
a little more soup where they were

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:51.640
distributing soup and there was some
left and they were going to give

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:56.230
everybody a little bit and the Jewish
police with their sticks and rubber

00:11:56.230 --> 00:12:04.360
hoses would beat them half cents
senseless and that's what happens when

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:10.480
the conditions are the way they were
then when a person becomes an animal and

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:14.800
loses self-respect that is willing to
get a beating and get beaten half

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:24.399
senseless just to get a little bit of
soup either you died or if you didn't

00:12:24.399 --> 00:12:30.639
die the Germans would come and take you
away the filth was just terrible in the

00:12:30.639 --> 00:12:35.620
camp in the barracks you had cops
I mean bunks where everybody slept on

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:40.720
straw there were no covers was no
pillows nothing you slept in your

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:44.850
clothes in the winters in the winter
because you're afraid to get cold

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:49.540
the only heating was a pot-bellied stove
in the middle of the barrack when

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:52.870
somebody stole some wood in the factory
or something then we had a fire

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:59.379
otherwise it was freezing and the only
warmth they had if you huddled together

00:12:59.379 --> 00:13:07.809
and they kept warm that way but nobody
would get undressed the lice were eating

00:13:07.809 --> 00:13:13.660
you up alive once a month you allowed to
take a shower and we had to walk to camp

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:21.339
a to go to the showers and that means a
10-mile walk 10 miles there and 10 miles

00:13:21.339 --> 00:13:27.220
back a lot of people were too weak to
walk and those people hardly ever took a

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:33.879
shower there was no hot water in the
washroom there was only cold and in the

00:13:33.879 --> 00:13:36.579
winter you were afraid to wash up
because there was nothing to wipe

00:13:36.579 --> 00:13:42.339
yourself with therefore you would walk
out in the cold winter and freeze to

00:13:42.339 --> 00:13:49.159
death

00:13:49.499 --> 00:13:56.589
and Henry gold brothers and both of his
parents were all killed in the gas

00:13:56.589 --> 00:14:08.740
chambers and from the Buchenwald report
which was translated and edited with an

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:14.230
introduction by David a Hackett I will
now describe the crematoriums in the

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:20.769
transports at the station two thousand
people got off the trains they had to

00:14:20.769 --> 00:14:25.660
throw away all their luggage afterward
the men and women were divided into two

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:32.230
groups at which the larger boys were
assigned to the group with the men then

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:36.730
that great devourer of Jews Mengele
drove buying a car seeking out the

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:41.769
strongest from each transport they
numbered around thirty out of two

00:14:41.769 --> 00:14:49.749
thousand the remainder were led away by
SS technical sergeant mole the officer

00:14:49.749 --> 00:14:54.910
of the crematorium the elderly were
loaded onto dump trucks and then dumped

00:14:54.910 --> 00:15:00.939
into burning trenches while still alive
the remainder were led to the gas

00:15:00.939 --> 00:15:08.559
chambers meanwhile new transports were
arriving in front of the gas chamber was

00:15:08.559 --> 00:15:15.129
a dressing room on its walls was written
in all languages put shoes into the

00:15:15.129 --> 00:15:20.559
cubbyholes and tie them together so you
will not lose them after the showers you

00:15:20.559 --> 00:15:28.059
will receive hot coffee here poor
victims undress themselves and went into

00:15:28.059 --> 00:15:33.309
the chamber there were three columns for
the ventilators through which the gas

00:15:33.309 --> 00:15:39.309
poured in a special work detail with
dungeons drove the people into the

00:15:39.309 --> 00:15:46.379
chamber when the room was full small
children were thrown in through a window

00:15:46.379 --> 00:15:52.059
sergeant mole grabbed infants by their
legs and smashed their skulls against

00:15:52.059 --> 00:15:59.410
the wall then the gas was led into the
chamber the lungs of the victim slowly

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:02.280
burst
and after three minutes a loud clamoring

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:09.300
could be heard then the chamber was open
and those who still showed signs of life

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:12.320
were beaten to death

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:22.410
the prisoners of the special work
details then cold corpses out took their

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:26.490
rings off and cut their hair which was
gathered up put in sacks and shipped to

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:35.130
factories then they arranged the corpses
in piles of ten each after sergeant mole

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:39.110
had counted them they were taken to the
ovens or if the crematoriums

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:46.470
crematoriums were insufficient thrown
into fire trenches once it happened that

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:50.460
a victim crawled out of a burning trench
he was beaten to death with truncheon

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:58.290
'he's on sergeant mole pull put a naked
woman in the trench and shot her in the

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:02.700
genitals
another time mold found a ring on a

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:08.670
member of the special work detail he
ordered nat naphtha poured over him and

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:16.260
had it lighted he hanged a man by his
hands and shot him until his arms were

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:25.050
torn through then he hanged him up by
the feet and repeated the process once

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:31.110
sergeant mole took a family of six first
and shot the youngest in the presence of

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:39.230
the rest then he shot the older ones and
finally their father and mother

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:56.450
and that's the testimony of john de
weiss who was 15 years old at Birkenau

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:11.720
so I think it's safe to say that this is
a situation that is definitely worse

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:29.180
than a nightmare and what possibly can
we take away from that I think the best

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:38.020
thing we can do is try and learn and I
think one of the best places to learn

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:43.550
and take lessons away from the Holocaust
is from the book that I started out with

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:56.080
today called man's search for meaning by
Viktor Frankl let's go to the book

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:06.190
let us take the case of a transport
which was officially announced to

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:10.810
transfer a certain number of prisoners
to another camp but it was fairly safe

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:15.700
to guess that its final destination
would be the gas chambers a selection of

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:20.530
sick or feeble prisoners incapable of
work would be sent to one of the big

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:26.620
central camps which were fitted with gas
chambers and crematoriums the selection

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:33.880
process was the signal for a free fight
among all the prisoners or of group

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:41.140
against group all that mattered was that
one's own name and that of one's friends

00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:46.210
were crossed off the list of victims
though everyone knew that for each man

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:53.440
saved another victim had to be found a
definite number of prisoners had to go

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:59.980
with each transport it did not really
matter which since each of them was

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:06.280
nothing but a number on their admission
to the camp all their documents had been

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:11.710
taken from them together with other
possessions each prisoner therefore had

00:20:11.710 --> 00:20:15.280
an opportunity to claim a fictitious
name a profession and for various

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:20.980
reasons many did this the authorities
were interested only in the captain's

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:26.230
numbers these numbers were often
tattooed on their skin and also had to

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:32.170
be sewn to a certain spot on their
trousers jacket or coat any guard who

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:36.480
wanted to make a charge against a
prisoner just glanced at his number and

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:44.170
how he dreaded those glances he never
asked for his name to return to the

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:47.890
convoy about the depart there was
neither time nor desire to consider

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:52.570
moral or ethical issues every man was
controlled by one thought only to keep

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:59.830
himself alive for the family waiting for
him at home and to save his friends with

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:05.650
no hesitation therefore he would arrange
for another prisoner another number to

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:13.420
take his place in the transport as I
have already mentioned the process of

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:18.460
selecting Kapos was a negative one
only the most brutal of the prisoners

00:21:18.460 --> 00:21:21.420
were chosen for this job

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:31.930
so Kappos or prisoners Jewish prisoners
that got selected to help run the prison

00:21:31.930 --> 00:21:39.040
camp and and capo I tried to find the
etymology of where that came from and

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:42.490
the best or the closest or the thing
that made the most sense to me from what

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:48.120
I read was was they had two words
combined together

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:58.150
comrade police then the in German does
got shortened to capital so some of

00:21:58.150 --> 00:22:02.620
these and you already heard one of those
earlier testimonies talking about the

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:08.830
Jewish police inside the prisons and how
they were just as bad as in many cases

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:15.430
as the Germans themselves back to the
book but apart from the selection of

00:22:15.430 --> 00:22:19.390
Kapos which is undertaken by the SS
there was a sort of self-selecting

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:27.490
process going on the whole time among
all the prisoners on the average only

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:32.650
those prisoners could keep alive who
after years of trekking from Camp to

00:22:32.650 --> 00:22:37.930
camp had lost all scruples in their
fight for existence they were prepared

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:43.690
to use every means honest and otherwise
even brutal force theft and betrayal of

00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:52.810
friends in order to save themselves will
you have come back by the aid of many

00:22:52.810 --> 00:23:02.800
lucky chances or miracles whatever one
may choose to call them we know the best

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:06.600
of us did not return

00:23:09.200 --> 00:23:14.100
so there's a dynamic that you don't
think of immediately when you think of

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:18.660
the concentration camps number one you
don't think about the kapos the Jewish

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:21.870
police that are forced into this
situation and then the other thing you

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:27.080
don't think about is this the fact that
the people that were going to survive

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:32.760
we're basically making it very broad say
but but you know from what Viktor Frankl

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:36.660
saying here it was the people that were
willing just to do anything to survive

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:41.010
including betraying their friends and
he's saying straight up we know the best

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:52.020
of us did not return and that is
something that you don't think about and

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:55.620
we probably don't think about it because
we don't want to think about it because

00:23:55.620 --> 00:24:01.950
when you start thinking about what would
you do to survive what would you do to

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:08.040
survive what would I do to survive and
that's a question I don't think a lot of

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:13.230
people want to know the answer to and
it's very easy to go through life and

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:16.520
never have to answer that question

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:28.020
talking a little bit now about what life
was like in the camp back to the book

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:31.140
somewhere it is said that man cannot
exist without sleep for more than a

00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:36.360
stated number of hours quite wrong I had
been convinced that there were certain

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:41.490
things I just could not do I could not
sleep without this or I could not live

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:45.690
without that or the other the first
night in Auschwitz

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:52.620
we slept in beds which were constructed
in tears on each tear measuring about 6

00:24:52.620 --> 00:25:00.240
and 1/2 to 8 feet slept 9 men directly
